@docs/spec/sigil-config.sigil from SpecWorkspaceConfig import {
  language version,
  source selection,
  workspace root,
}

component SpecLanguageReference {
  goal {
    Define the Sigil language so that every tool parses, resolves, and reports a design the same way.
  }

  constraints {
    A tool must not report a facet as satisfied when its meaning cannot be established under the interpretation boundary.

    A tag import must not create a namespace, a re-export, or a runtime call.

    A tag import must resolve only to a source that the source selection includes, by a path inside the workspace root. SpecLanguageReference depends on SpecWorkspaceConfig for the language version, the source selection, and the workspace root.

    A tool that cannot process the language version must report the diagnostic code SIGIL_UNSUPPORTED_VERSION and must not interpret the source under another language version.

    A formatter must not rewrite a partial result as if recovery had made it valid.

    A tool must not choose between the grammar and the reference when they conflict, because the conflict is a specification defect.

    Wrapping to the formatting width must not split a tag identity or change a facet.
  }

  interface {
    SpecLanguageReference provides the *Sigil language*, whose sources every tool parses, resolves, and reports under one language version.

    SpecLanguageReference provides the *grammar*, the EBNF productions that define syntax, while the reference supplies the lexical algorithms and contextual conditions.

    SpecLanguageReference provides seven *contract section* kinds in one canonical order, and every component must have a goal and an interface.

    SpecLanguageReference provides the *facet*, the finest authored unit, which keeps its owning component, contract section, and source range.

    SpecLanguageReference provides the *tag identity*, an exact, case-sensitive name owned by one component and declared in one source. A duplicate or ambiguous tag identity has no winner by source order.

    SpecLanguageReference provides the *source document*, one UTF-8 Sigil source whose lines, declarations, and facets are kept as written.

    SpecLanguageReference provides *embedded content*, one fenced payload that follows its introducing prose directly. The *opening fence* must stay adjacent to that prose, and an *unclosed fence* keeps the rest of the source as payload.

    SpecLanguageReference provides *resolution*, which settles each name and import to one owner and leaves an ambiguous name unresolved.

    SpecLanguageReference provides *name matching*, the rules that find a name in facet prose: eligible regions, word boundaries, connector runs, and the longest match.

    SpecLanguageReference provides the *tag import*, an explicit selection of a tag identity from one provider component in one source file. Only a tag identity that the provider interface evidences is importable.

    SpecLanguageReference provides the *source range*, a half-open span of original UTF-8 bytes taken before formatting or name processing.

    SpecLanguageReference provides the *diagnostic code* for each language or workspace violation, with its stage, severity, and related locations, so every tool reports the same failure the same way.

    SpecLanguageReference provides the *partial result* that a parser may return for invalid input. It keeps the invalidity and the source range and is never acceptance.

    SpecLanguageReference provides the *formatting width*, at most 79 content characters per prose line, not counting indentation or link destinations, wrapped only at whitespace.

    SpecLanguageReference provides the *interpretation boundary*. Parsing, validation, and resolution establish structure and ownership, and none of them establishes runtime behavior, conformance, or a test result.
  }

  decisions {
    The reference and the grammar form one specification, so that core, the language server, the CLI, and the skill language pack read one definition.
  }
}

