@packages/sigilc/vocabulary.sigil from SigilClaimsVocabulary import { AcceptedVocabulary, AtomDiscipline }
@packages/sigilc/compiler.sigil from SigilSemanticCompiler import { CompilerCommands }

component SigilComputedClaims {
  goal {
    Derive design results from caller supplied readings under AcceptedVocabulary, and return the computed report and evidence for review.
  }

  constraints {
    *ClaimsCommands* CompilerCommands The external interpretation is supplied by the caller; sigilc launches no model or worker. Reusing a stored interpretation reuses caller data. A check is incomplete when a design unit has no valid reading or an import cannot be resolved.

    *ReturnedClaims* Apply AtomDiscipline to the complete artifact and AcceptedVocabulary to row names. Accepted claims do not prove the source prose or interpretation is true. A defective data row can make its unit unread without discarding other valid units.

    *SectionAwareClosure* The source trees are read-only. Laws are tool-owned; findings and stored readings are written under the selected claims store.
  }

  cases {
    ReturnedClaims A rule beside a valid row refuses the complete artifact under AtomDiscipline. A claim naming an entity outside its Facet's presented names refuses that unit. A malformed row refuses only its Facet or Logic section when other rows remain valid.

    SectionAwareClosure A required Constraints claim raises an obligation; the same claim in Decisions raises none. A reading row records a read Facet with no commitment. An uninterpreted required Facet remains a named gap.
  }

  interface {
    *CompiledGuidance* Publish accepted row forms, contract roles, examples, and refusal rules to the external interpreter. Guidance is compiled with the executable and its fingerprint identifies the guidance and claim vocabulary. Extracted copies are written only to a named path outside the workspace under check.

    *InterpretationRequest* Project one design source and the interface Facets of imported components. Present each source Facet with its role, prose, resolved names, stable identity, and a short handle. Preserve the source's physical Facet order, including Logic Facets. Bind the request to source interfaces, guidance, and vocabulary generation. Preparation asks only for stale units; Logic is requested as a section flow while each Facet keeps its own row identity.

    ReturnedClaims Accept only data rows described by AcceptedVocabulary. Mint claim and flow identities in the tool, fill contract roles from the source, and resolve names only against the selected source and imported interfaces. Compare an optional repeat interpretation with the first as a stability observation; the first remains the basis of findings.

    SectionAwareClosure Saturate tool-authored laws over accepted claims to derive commitments, contradictions, reachability, ownership conflicts, and unmet obligations. Decisions claims are retained but derive no commitments; assumed claims raise an obligation to state their basis. Logic steps form a flow graph per component section, with explicit ends and guards. Report unreachable steps, defective rows, and unguarded flows without treating model flow readings as design-level contradictions.

    *ComputedFindings* Report findings with their producing law, supporting claims or flow steps, and Facet spans tied to source-byte digests. Identify unread units, unresolved imports, warnings, and interpretation disagreements. A linked check reports each finding to every source that authored a named part. Identical source inputs, readings, and runtime identity reproduce the report.

    *JudgmentContext* Write one entry per selected source unit, with its interpretation, derived facts, obligation state, provenance, and gaps. Write a prose-free review view and evidence mapping aliases to the exact source bytes used by the linked check.

    ClaimsCommands Provide prepare, ingest, check, and guidance extraction. Preparation writes requests for external interpretation; ingest validates a binding and stores accepted readings; check links stored readings and runs laws. Exit zero for completed pass or warning, one for gate failure or incomplete check, two for usage or refused input, and three for operational failure. A Disjoint design or saturation limit breach fails the gate.
  }
}

