  room,
  room owner,
  archived room,
  room timezone,
}
@availability.sigil from Availability import { open time, blackout }
@booking.sigil from Booking import {
  booking request,
  renter,
  pending request,
  confirmed booking,
  outside-availability flag,
}
@shared.sigil from SharedKernel import { clock, 30-minute grid }

component Calendar {
  goal {
          Show each viewer what a room offers and holds over a date range, revealing only what that viewer may see.
  }

  logic {
          Building a *calendar view* takes six steps. Step one takes a room, a *viewer*, a local date range in the room timezone, and the clock. Step two rejects a range that breaks the 31-day bound. Step three reads the open time for the range and, only when the viewer is the room owner, the blackout dates. Step four reads the booking request items of the room that touch the range, which are its confirmed booking and pending request items. Step five projects each item by the role of the viewer in a pure step, so masking is testable without a database. Step six marks each slot as a *requestable slot* or not and returns the calendar view to the caller.

          A requestable slot lies inside open time, overlaps no confirmed booking, starts after the clock and within Booking's start limit, is not in the second occurrence of a fall-back day's repeated hour, and belongs to a room that is not an archived room and whose viewer is not the room owner. Range-level rules stay with Booking's submission.

          A confirmed booking or pending request that overlaps the range is returned clipped to the range for display, and it carries its true start and end.

          Refreshing the *owner digest* of a room takes two steps. Step one reads the confirmed booking and pending request items of the room. Step two compares the owner digest of the room with the digest kept from the previous refresh.
  }

  constraints {
          A calendar view must span at most 31 days.

          For a viewer who is not the room owner, a calendar view must show another renter's confirmed booking only as a *booked block* with no renter identity, and must show none of another renter's pending request.

          For a viewer who is not the room owner, a calendar view must mark that viewer's own pending request and confirmed booking as theirs.

          For the room owner, a calendar view shows the open time, each blackout, each confirmed booking with its renter identity, each pending request, and each confirmed booking that carries an outside-availability flag.

          Calendar requires the *renter display name* of each renter from Identity, so the room owner's view can label every pending request and confirmed booking with a name.

          Calendar owns no stored data, and it must not cache a read of a confirmed booking or pending request.

          Calendar depends on Booking, Availability, and Rooms, and on SharedKernel for the clock.
  }

  cases {
          Given Ana holds a confirmed booking, a calendar view for Ben, who is not the room owner, shows a booked block with no name and none of Ana's pending request.

          A viewer who is not the room owner sees their own pending request and confirmed booking marked as theirs.

          The room owner sees the renter identity, each pending request, each blackout, and the outside-availability flag.

          A calendar view of a range of 32 days is rejected.

          A slot covered by a confirmed booking, a slot starting before the clock, and a slot 181 days ahead are all not requestable. Every slot on the room owner's own view and on an archived room is not requestable.

          A confirmed booking that spans the start of the range is returned clipped for display with its real start preserved.

          A calendar view of a spring-forward day in America/New_York renders 23 hours of slots, and one of a fall-back day renders 25, where the second occurrence of the repeated hour is shown but is not a requestable slot.

          Every slot is aligned to the 30-minute grid in the room timezone.
  }

  interface {
          Calendar provides a calendar view of a room for a viewer over a local date range of up to 31 days, in room-local time labelled with the room timezone.

          Calendar provides the calendar view of an archived room by direct link, with no requestable slot in it.

          Calendar provides open time and each requestable slot so a renter can pick a range only from those.
  }

  decisions {
          The room owner sees everything and other viewers see anonymous booked blocks, chosen over one shared view and over full transparency, because owners need a triage view and renters should not see each other.

          Identity masking sits in a pure projection so it can be unit-tested without a database.
  }
}

component Identity {
  goal {
    Establish who is acting on every page and action, with one account type and
    email and password sign-in, so the rest of Slotted never guesses.
  }

  state {
    Identity owns each *user account*, which has one email and one password
    credential and no stored role.

    Identity owns each *session* created at sign-in and ended at sign-out.
  }

  logic {
    Resolving the *signed-in user* takes four steps. Step one reads the session
    from the request headers. Step two looks up the user account that the session
    belongs to. Step three returns the signed-in user, or returns no user when the
    session is missing or invalid. Step four turns a missing user into an
    unauthenticated refusal at the requireUser gate, and otherwise hands the
    signed-in user to the calling page or action.
  }

  constraints {
    The optimistic redirect on the session cookie is not a security boundary. Only
    requireUser may decide whether a signed-in user exists for a page or action.

    Module commands and queries must not read the session themselves. They take the
    signed-in user as an explicit actor.

    A user account must not carry a role. Owning and renting come from a user's
    relationship to rooms and requests.
  }

  cases {
    Signing up and then signing in creates a session, and resolving the
    signed-in user from that session's headers returns the user.

    Resolving the signed-in user with no session or an invalid one returns no user,
    and requireUser then refuses as unauthenticated.

    Submitting a form after clearing the session changes nothing and is refused.

    Signing out ends the session, and the next page load redirects to sign-in.
  }

  interface {
    Identity lets a person sign up, sign in, and sign out with email and password
    on a user account.

    Identity provides requireUser, which returns the signed-in user for the current
    session or refuses as unauthenticated. Every page and action except sign-up and
    sign-in calls it.

    Identity provides resolution of the session from request headers outside
    Next.js, so the signed-in user can be tested without a running app.
  }

  decisions {
    Better Auth backs Identity because Auth.js is security-fixes-only and Lucia is
    deprecated.

    Roles come from the relationship, not the account, so one person can own and
    rent without two accounts.
  }
}

