@identity.sigil from Identity import { signed-in user }

component Rooms {
  goal {
    Let any signed-in user list a room for others to rent, keep the set of rooms browsable, and give every room-scoped change one serialized path.
  }

  state {
    Rooms owns each *room*, which has a name, description, capacity, location text, and a *room timezone* that is an IANA zone required at creation. Capacity is informational and a request carries no party size.

    Every room has exactly one *room owner*, the signed-in user who created it.

    A room is either listed or an *archived room*. Rooms owns the archived room mark: a room carries it from archive until unarchive.
  }

  logic {
    Creating a room takes three steps. Step one requires a signed-in user. Step two validates the room timezone against the IANA zone list and rejects an unknown zone. Step three records the signed-in user as the room owner and returns the new room.

    Running a change under the *room lock* takes three steps. Step one opens a transaction and locks the row of the room. Step two passes the transaction to the change. Step three commits and returns the result, or rolls back if the change fails.
  }

  constraints {
    Only the room owner may edit a room, archive it, unarchive it, or change its room timezone.

    Every room-scoped mutation, including availability edits and booking status changes made by other modules, must run inside the room lock. Changes to different rooms must not block each other.

    A *transactional entry* is a second entry beside a module public entry that runs inside a transaction its caller opened and that only Booking may import. Rooms exposes one that runs inside the room lock, and Rooms must expose the archive, unarchive, and room timezone mutations only through its transactional entry, and Rooms must not import Booking.

    Rooms depends on Identity and requires the signed-in user to record a room owner.
  }

  cases {
    Creating a room with an invalid room timezone is rejected.

    Editing a room by a signed-in user who is not its room owner is refused.

    The room list excludes every archived room and includes rooms from every room owner.

    Unarchiving an archived room returns it to the room list.

    Two concurrent changes under the room lock on one room run one after the other, while changes on different rooms do not block each other.
  }

  interface {
    Rooms lets a signed-in user create a room, and lets the room owner edit its name, description, capacity, and location text.

    Rooms provides the *room list* of every room that is not an archived room to any signed-in user.

    Rooms provides archive, unarchive, and room timezone change of a room through its transactional entry, for Booking's workflows to use.

    Rooms provides the room lock, which opens a transaction on one room and hands it to a change, for Booking and the commands of Rooms to use.

    Rooms provides the room owner of a room so other modules can authorize a signed-in user against it.

    Rooms provides the room timezone of a room so other modules can read a range in it.

    Rooms provides each room owner the list of every room that owner created, including each archived room, so the owner can reach an archived room to unarchive it.
  }

  decisions {
    The room timezone is locked while upcoming items exist because no booking should ever move in time. That check lives in Booking, which is why Rooms never imports it.

    A row lock in Postgres serializes room changes because the no-overlap rule and exact automatic declines need one ordering per room.
  }
}

